<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CPPCC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference is a political advisory body of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, bringing together the Communist Party, the minor parties and other groups under the party's leadership. Its first session in September 1949 acted as a constituent assembly, adopting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-program">Common Program</a> and electing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> as chairman of the new government, and it still meets each year alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chinese-peoples-political-consultative-conference.jpg" alt="Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_People%27s_Political_Consultative_Conference_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The conference has roots before the People's Republic. During negotiations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> in 1945, the two parties agreed in the Double Tenth Agreement to open multiparty talks on post-war political reforms through a Political Consultative Conference. The National Government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> organised the first one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> from 10 to 31 January 1946, with representatives of the Kuomintang, the Communist Party and other parties. It ended in failure, and the Communist Party withdrew. On 1 May 1948, after major successes in the civil war, the party invited other parties, popular organisations and community leaders to form a new conference to discuss a new state and a new coalition government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In September 1949, with the Communist Party in control of most of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the new conference met with delegates from friendly parties and was renamed the People's Political Consultative Conference. On 29 September 1949 its first plenary session unanimously adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-program">Common Program</a> as the basic political programme of the country. It approved the new national anthem, flag, capital city and state name, and elected the first government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. From 1949 to 1954 it acted as the de facto legislature and issued nearly 3,500 laws; the Constitution of 1954 transferred legislative functions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a>.</p><p>During the Hundred Flowers Campaign of 1956–1957 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> encouraged members to speak about the party's shortcomings, but those who did faced heavy criticism or imprisonment in labour camps in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-rightist-campaign">Anti-Rightist Campaign</a> that followed. Like most institutions, the conference was effectively decimated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>. It was revived at the first session of its 5th National Committee, from 24 February to 8 March 1974, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> was elected chairman. Rules issued in 1983 limited Communist Party members to 40 percent of its membership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The conference consists of a National Committee and regional committees at the provincial, prefecture and county levels. Its members come from the Communist Party and its people's organisations, from eight other parties, and from nominally independent members, with seats also reserved for overseas delegates and for deputies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. National Committee members serve five-year terms. The National Committee normally meets in March, at the same time as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a>, and the two meetings are together called the Two Sessions. The conference advises government bodies and submits proposals but has no legislative power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Chinese People’s Political Consultative Conference</b> adopted a Common Program in September, and on 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic from the Gate of Heavenly Peace in Beijing, which again became the capital, while the Nationalist government withdrew to Taiwan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1951, meeting Wang Shen at the <b>Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference</b>, Mao Zedong gave him a signed copy of his newly published Selected Works.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland</a></li></ul></section>
